        <h2 id="sound-isolation-h" class={h2}>
          Understanding Sound Isolation: Airborne vs. Impact Noise
        </h2>
        <p class={lede}>
          To choose the right soundproofing material, it helps to understand the two main ways
          sound travels through a building:
        </p>
        <dl class="mt-8 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
          {
            noiseTypes.map((it) => (
              <div class="rounded border border-industrial-300 bg-white p-6">
                <span
                  class="grid h-11 w-11 place-items-center rounded bg-industrial-100 text-amber-700 ring-1 ring-industrial-300"
                  aria-hidden="true"
                >
                  {it.icon === 'airborne' && (
                    <svg
                      viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                      fill="none"
                      stroke="currentColor"
                      stroke-width="1.75"
                      class="h-6 w-6"
                    >
                      <path
                        stroke-linecap="round"
                        stroke-linejoin="round"
                        d="M6.75 8.25H4.5a1.5 1.5 0 0 0-1.5 1.5v4.5a1.5 1.5 0 0 0 1.5 1.5h2.25l4.28 3.53a.75.75 0 0 0 1.22-.58V5.3a.75.75 0 0 0-1.22-.58L6.75 8.25Zm9.53-.53a5.25 5.25 0 0 1 0 8.56m2.65-11.2a9 9 0 0 1 0 13.84"
                      />
                    </svg>
                  )}
                  {it.icon === 'impact' && (
                    <svg
                      viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                      fill="none"
                      stroke="currentColor"
                      stroke-width="1.75"
                      class="h-6 w-6"
                    >
                      <path
                        stroke-linecap="round"
                        stroke-linejoin="round"
                        d="M12 3v9m0 0 3.5-3.5M12 12 8.5 8.5M4.5 20.25h15M6 15l-1.75 3.5M18 15l1.75 3.5"
                      />
                    </svg>
                  )}
                </span>
                <dt class="mt-3 font-heading text-lg font-semibold text-charcoal">{it.term}</dt>
                <dd class="mt-2 text-sm text-charcoal-600">{it.desc}</dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-charcoal-600">
          Concrete floor slabs in modern high-rise towers are very good at blocking airborne
          noise because they are thick and dense. However, concrete naturally transfers impact
          noise very quickly. Our recycled rubber acoustic underlays and screed isolation mats
          act as a shock absorber. They decouple the finished floor from the concrete slab,
          stopping impact energy before it becomes sound.
        </p>

        <figure class="m-0 mt-10">
          <img
            src={`${IMG}/airborne-vs-impact-noise-sound-transfer-diagram.jpg`}
            alt="Diagram comparing airborne noise and impact noise travelling through a concrete floor slab between building levels, with a recycled rubber acoustic underlay absorbing the impact energy."
            title="Airborne vs. impact noise transfer through a floor slab - Rukn Al Zain"
            width="1408"
            height="768"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="h-auto w-full rounded border border-industrial-300"
          />
          <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-charcoal-500">
            How airborne and impact noise move between floors, and where a resilient rubber layer
            interrupts the impact path.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* Meeting Dubai Acoustic & Building Standards */}
    <section aria-labelledby="dubai-standards-h" class={`${sectionShell} bg-white`}>
