        <h2 id="dubai-standards-h" class={h2}>Meeting Dubai Acoustic &amp; Building Standards</h2>

        <div class="mt-8 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:items-start">
          {/* Text — left */}
          <div>
            <p class="text-charcoal-600">
              High-rise buildings in Dubai feature dense concrete structures. Concrete blocks
              structural strength, but it easily transfers impact sound—like heavy footsteps or
              dropped items—straight through floors.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4 text-charcoal-600">
              Dubai Municipality enforces strict indoor noise limits under the <strong
                class="font-semibold text-charcoal">Al Sa’fat Green Building System</strong> and the
              <strong class="font-semibold text-charcoal">Dubai Building Code</strong>. Developers
              and contractors must hit target ratings for Impact Insulation Class (IIC) and Sound
              Transmission Class (STC).
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4 text-charcoal-600">
              Rukn Al Zain acoustic solutions cushion your floor slabs. They absorb energy before
              it turns into noise, helping your project pass acoustic testing smoothly.
            </p>
          </div>

          {/* Image — right */}
          <figure class="m-0">
            <img
              src={`${IMG}/dubai-building-code-concrete-subfloor-inspection.jpg`}
              alt="Engineer inspecting a concrete subfloor before acoustic underlay installation in a Dubai high-rise."
              title="Concrete subfloor inspection ahead of acoustic underlay installation, Dubai"
              width="1376"
              height="768"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              class="h-auto w-full rounded border border-industrial-300"
            />
            <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-charcoal-500">
              Smooth subfloor concrete ready for acoustic membrane laying to pass strict sound
              inspections.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

